-- Passage 1: Harvesting Cork Without Felling the Oak

Harvesting has two clocks. The first concerns the tree''s size: in Portugal, the trunk must reach a circumference of at least 70 centimetres, measured 1.3 metres above the ground, before its first stripping. This usually takes about 25 years. The second clock is the interval between harvests. Portuguese rules forbid stripping a tree again in less than nine years, even if its bark appears ready sooner. The first crop, called virgin cork, is irregular and hard. It can serve uses such as flooring or insulation, but is unsuitable for quality bottle stoppers.

The second crop, taken at least nine years later, is more regular yet still generally unsuitable for those stoppers. It is from the third and later crops that cork with the desired structure is usually obtained. Thus a young tree that has just reached the minimum size does not immediately become a source of stopper-grade material. Nor does a thick-looking trunk alone tell a worker when it may next be harvested. Both the stage in the tree''s harvesting history and the time since its previous stripping matter. The system depends on skilled cutting as much as on patience: careless removal could damage the living tree on which future crops depend.', false)
