-- Passage 2: Patterns on a Marbling Bath

The Turkish tradition known as ebru has long been associated with the decoration of books and calligraphic pages. The Metropolitan Museum of Art describes marbled borders around calligraphy in a manuscript album, where the colours frame rather than replace the writing. Patterns may include flowers, speckled areas and combed lines. A comb can turn a field of floating colour into a regular design, but an uncombed pattern can also be valued for its movement. In either case, the pattern exists on the bath before it appears on the paper.

Marbling has appeared in more than one cultural tradition. Japanese suminagashi, meaning “floating ink”, also transfers marks from water to paper, although its familiar rings of ink are produced differently from many thickened-bath patterns. European bookbinders used marbled sheets for covers and endpapers, the leaves joining a book''s pages to its cover. These shared uses do not prove that every maker followed the same recipe. Describing a sheet simply as “marbled” identifies the broad method and visual effect, but it does not by itself reveal whether a comb was used or which liquid and colours produced it.', false)
;

