-- Passage 3: Working to the Rhythm of a Tide Mill

This arrangement differs from a river mill fed continuously by a stream. A tide mill does not simply run whenever waves reach the shore. It must first fill its pond and then wait for a useful difference between pond and sea levels. Once released, the stored water gradually loses its height advantage, so work is limited by the available head and the amount in the pond. The tide will refill the pond in a later cycle, but the timing of useful power changes with the tide rather than following a fixed daily working hour.

Historic England identifies tide mills as a distinct form of watermill, while noting that much of the wheel and mill machinery resembles that of other watermills. At Totton and Eling in Hampshire, a surviving mill stands on the seaward side of a dam across the mouth of Bartley Water. Historic England records a working waterwheel, millstones and sluice gear there. These remains make the arrangement visible: the dam and gates manage the supply, and the familiar wheel and stones perform the grinding. To understand the mill, therefore, it is necessary to consider both its ordinary milling equipment and the changing water levels outside.', false)
;

