-- Passage 4: Fire in the Walls of Scottish Hillforts

The White Caterthun in Angus has a great stone fort with evidence of vitrification. Its neighbouring Brown Caterthun has a more complicated sequence. Excavation found parts of its earthworks had been maintained, burned and rebuilt over time. Historic Environment Scotland notes that both places had various earthworks and that their exact functions remain debated. They may have served defensive and ceremonial roles. The visible evidence supports a history more complicated than a single episode of construction followed by abandonment.

Another Scottish example, the dun at Rahoy, offers a closer view of a burned wall. A dun is a small fortified settlement. Excavators found cavities where timber beams had once passed through sections of vitrified material. This helps explain a physical route from timber-laced stonework to fused rock when the timber burned. It does not identify the person who set the fire. At Rahoy, only part of the interior was fully excavated, so any account of activity across the entire site must be cautious. Read together, the sites show how a material trace can reveal a process while leaving the human intention behind it open to further evidence.', false)
