-- Passage 5: Catching Water from Coastal Fog

Researchers at MIT, working with colleagues in Chile, examined three features of a mesh: the thickness of its strands, the gaps between them and the coating on their surfaces. They found that commonly used plastic meshes had strands and gaps too large for efficient collection under mild fog conditions. A finer experimental mesh was predicted to capture a larger share of the water in air passing through it. Its coating helped droplets slide down promptly into a gutter rather than remain on the strands. These findings describe an improvement under particular conditions, not a guarantee of a fixed daily water supply everywhere.

The research took place in a context where coastal fog can reach very dry land near Chile''s Atacama Desert. The team installed test screens on hilltops north of Santiago to study water yield and durability over time. Wind strength and fog density still matter: a mesh cannot collect droplets that never reach it. The researchers also discussed placing several meshes one behind another to intercept water left in the airflow. Yet extra screens would have to be assessed as a system; the passage gives no measured annual yield for such a series. The attraction of the approach is its simple physical mechanism, while its practical output depends on local conditions and the design used.', false)
